      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 78 · Finegal or Abraham? · pp. 176–177", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "In a single moment of pivotal decision, Florence rejects the marriage proposal of young Finegal O’Flahertie and chooses to become Mrs. Abraham Stoker instead.", page: "176" },
        { text: "Those who knew him best did not agree.", page: "177" }
      ],
      idea: `Roy tells this one like a mystery and holds the famous names back until the last paragraph, so if you haven't read pages 176–177 yet, read them first.

Florence Balcombe has two suitors in Ireland. **Finegal O'Flahertie** is the poetic, colorful son of a flamboyant poet-activist mother who taught him to treat life as a performance. **Abraham Stoker** was a sickly boy who disciplined himself into an athlete and now works as a courthouse file clerk. Florence's father, a lieutenant colonel, much prefers the clerk. In one decision, Florence turns Finegal down and marries Abraham.

Heartbroken, Finegal goes to London, wins a major poetry prize and decides to become a star. He turns everything into theater: clothes from theater costumers instead of tailors, a cab hailed just to cross the street. Soon all England is talking about him. Abraham, a little intimidated, publishes a book of his own: a manual on the duties of petty-court clerks. It goes nowhere.

Then the reversal. A powerful, macho aristocrat (the man who wrote boxing's rulebook; history knows him as the Marquess of Queensberry, though Roy never names him) accuses Finegal of homosexuality. Finegal sues, loses, and in 1895 is sentenced to two years of hard labor. Out of prison, he learns that Florence's husband, **Bram** Stoker, has written another book: *Dracula*, which by Roy's account soon outsold everything but the Bible. Finegal is **Oscar** Fingal O'Flahertie Wills Wilde (Roy spells it Finegal). He dies in a rented Paris room in 1900, at 46. Doctors blamed an ear infection; Roy's last line hints that his friends believed something else killed him: a broken spirit.

**The lesson Roy doesn't state, said plainly:**
- **A life built as a performance belongs to its audience.** Oscar chose fame and, failing that, notoriety (the sidebar quotes his youthful vow). When the audience turned, there was nothing underneath to stand on.
- **You can't judge the race from the starting line.** The "dull" clerk's first book was a flop about paperwork; his second is still in print. That's Chapter 72's "think big, start small" in action.
- **One pivotal decision redirects lives.** Florence's choice is the hinge of the story, a theme Part Four keeps returning to (Chapters 80, 81, 90).

> **Principle:** Attention is rented; character and craft are owned. Spectacle gets you noticed fast, but only substance keeps you standing when the crowd turns.

**Craft note:** Roy hides the famous names until the end, and he'll do it again with Chanel, Rothschild, the tuxedo, Lewis Carroll, Twain, Stevenson and Jefferson. Withholding the name makes the reader work, then pays them off with a surprise at the close. That's surprising Broca (Chapters 19–20) and a strong last mental image.`,
      keyPoints: [
        "Florence's single decision (the clerk over the poet) is the hinge of both men's lives.",
        "Oscar Wilde built his identity on performance and fame; when public opinion turned, it destroyed him.",
        "Bram Stoker's first book flopped; a later one was *Dracula*. Dull starts are normal.",
        "Roy withholds the famous names until the end, a reveal that surprises Broca and leaves a strong last mental image.",
        "Attention is not a foundation. What you are when the crowd leaves is."
      ],
      why: `ZDub can buy attention for PMT in an afternoon; what keeps the business standing is what happens after the click (the class, the follow-up, the reviews). And for Zach, a quiet, unglamorous first year of real work beats a loud launch with nothing underneath.`,
