        body: `Same facts, different order. The before version leads with the label and leaks the ending. The after version holds the identity back and makes the viewer lean in: Roy's Wilde/Stoker move. Use a real student's story with permission (Dana is a placeholder) and keep every detail true; the reveal only works if nothing is invented. And note the chapter's other lesson: Dana's win is substance, not spectacle. An ad that promises spectacle the class can't deliver is Oscar's lawsuit waiting to happen.`
