      title: "Pull the Trigger and Ride the Bullet: decide, then own the result",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 81 · Pull the Trigger and Ride the Bullet · pp. 182–183", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Whenever there is a decision to be made, make it! Don’t be a hand-wringing weasel.", page: "183" },
        { text: "Each and every day of your life, you get one more chance to get it right.", page: "183" }
      ],
      idea: `Harry Truman's epigraph sets the tone: Moses didn't take a poll in Egypt, and leadership is about right and wrong, not the public opinion of the moment.

Then Roy introduces his best friend, **Tony** (the same Tony who shows up in Chapters 72, 82 and 84): a recluse so private that Roy's staff thinks he's imaginary, a man who has never held a regular job. For twenty-five years Roy has watched him buy and sell cars as a teenager, run a small oil company and wholesale fine furniture, making and losing thousands along the way. He's also watched the disasters: Tony's first wife ran off with the carnival, the state auctioned his paid-off house for back taxes, Roy had to bail him out of jail after Tony held a gun to his ex-wife's boyfriend's head, and a bad short sale on a day trade cost him a quarter of a million dollars.

Roy is blunt that Tony has done some extraordinarily stupid things. He is not holding up Tony's choices as a model. He's holding up **one trait**: in all those years Roy has never heard Tony whine about how things turned out or point a finger at anyone but himself. Where most people would wail "why me?", Tony shrugs at the sky and says he pulled the trigger and rode the bullet.

From that, Roy draws the rule: when there's a decision to make, make it. Do you have the courage of your convictions, or do you waffle and hop from one foot to the other? Then he offers the reassurance that makes decisiveness safe. Yes, tomorrow is shaped by today's choices, but the day after tomorrow is shaped by tomorrow's. Every day you get another chance to get it right, so no single decision is the last word.

Chapter 72 adds Tony's follow-up: if the first bullet misses, pull the trigger and ride another one.

> **Principle:** Decisiveness plus total ownership. Decide, accept the consequences without blaming anyone, and use tomorrow's decision to correct today's.

**For ZDub:** Agencies are blame machines: the algorithm, the season, the client's slow phone answering. The owner who says "that was my call, here's what I learned, here's what changes Monday" becomes the one clients trust. The rule also runs Zach's daily work: kill the losing ad, send the proposal, raise the price. No hand-wringing. Tomorrow brings another decision to fix today's.`,
      keyPoints: [
        "Tony made plenty of bad choices; Roy admires only his refusal to whine or blame.",
        "When there's a decision to make, make it. Don't wring your hands.",
        "Today's choices shape tomorrow, but tomorrow's shape the day after: you always get another chance.",
        "Leadership isn't polling (Truman); it's deciding what's right and owning it.",
        "If the first bullet misses, ride another one (Chapter 72)."
      ],
      why: `Clients pay an agency to make calls and own them. "My call, my lesson, my fix" builds more trust than any single good month, and it keeps Zach moving instead of frozen.`,
      example: {
        title: "The ownership report vs. the blame report",
        before: `"Hi Chris, leads were down this week. Meta has been really unstable lately and costs are up everywhere, plus it's the slow season, so it's not really anything on our end. Hopefully it picks up!"`,
        after: `"Hi Chris, leads dropped from 14 to 8 this week. That's on me: I let our best ad run two weeks past when it started tiring. I've paused it, and two new hooks go live tomorrow morning. I'll send you the first 48-hour numbers Thursday."`,
        body: `Both reports could mention the same facts. The first points the finger outward and asks Chris to hope. The second pulls the trigger (a clear decision already made) and rides the bullet (owns the result). Notice it doesn't grovel, either: Tony shrugs and moves on. (The numbers are illustrations; use the real ones.)`
      },
