      source: [{ doc: "Magical Worlds of the Wizard of Ads (Roy H. Williams, 2001)", section: "Chapter 87 · The Value of Heroes · pp. 194–195", who: "Roy H. Williams" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Most people assume that legends, myths, and stories of heroes are simply the byproducts of great civilizations, but I’m convinced that they are the cause of them.", page: "194" },
        { text: "It’s no secret that people will usually do in reality what they have seen themselves do in their minds.", page: "195" }
      ],
      idea: `As late as 1937, the sun really did never set on the British Empire: tiny England held possessions in all 24 time zones. Everyone knows the British conquered much of the world. Roy asks the more interesting question: what made them believe they could?

For the first thousand years after Christ, he says, only Greece and Rome were telling stories of heroes and champions. England was a dreary little island of "rejects, castoffs, barbarians, and losers" (Roy's words, for effect). Then, in 1136, a Welsh monk named Geoffrey of Monmouth, hoping to give his people some pride, published *History of the Kings of Britain*: a detailed account of seventeen glorious centuries of English deeds. Not one word of it was true. Geoffrey invented Merlin, Guinevere, Arthur and the Knights of the Round Table. (The sidebar adds that Malory's famous *Le Morte d'Arthur*, which most people take as the source of the legend, came 349 years later and was built on Geoffrey.)

The effect: a sad little island began to see itself as a just and magnificent nation. First in its mind, then in the mirror.

Roy then flips the usual assumption. Most people think legends and hero stories are **byproducts** of great civilizations. He's convinced they're the **cause**: the mightiest civilizations are the ones with larger-than-life role models who inspired ordinary people to rise up and do amazing things. His one-line psychology: people usually do in reality what they have first seen themselves do in their minds. Gerald W. Johnson's sidebar adds that heroes are created by popular demand, sometimes out of scanty materials.

This is Part One's thesis applied to a nation: reality begins with imagination (Chapter 5), and words speak worlds into existence (Chapter 1). The Arthur thread comes back in Chapter 100, when a real-life Round Table moment happens to Roy in Guatemala.

> **Principle:** Identity comes before behavior. Give people a story in which they're the hero, and they start living up to it.

**For ZDub, two ways:**
- **In ads:** PMT's ads should let the prospect see *themselves* riding. The hero is the student, not the instructor or the school. People do what they've already rehearsed in their minds, so show them the scene they'll walk into.
- **For Zach:** what story does he tell himself about ZDub? "A guy with one client" and "the agency that's going to fill the calendars of Sacramento's best local businesses" can describe the same facts, but they produce different Mondays. (Geoffrey's history was false. Zach's should be true, just told as a story worth living up to.)`,
